Paper:
Prompting Techniques for Secure Code Generation: A Systematic Investigation. Catherine Tony, Nicolás E. Díaz Ferreyra, Markus Mutas, Salem Dhiff, Riccardo Scandariato

This paper investigates how prompt engineering techniques affect the security of code generated by LLMs, addressing a notable gap in prior research where prompting methods and code-security outcomes have rarely been studied together. LLMs like GPT-3/3.5/4 are widely used for natural-language–to-code generation, yet existing work shows they often produce insecure code due to reliance on flawed open-source training data and developers’ over-trust in AI assistants (e.g., insecure solutions observed in Perry et al.’s user study). The authors conduct the first systematic literature review (SLR) to identify prompting techniques applicable to code generation. From 358 screened papers, they identify 15 techniques, grouped into five categories: root (e.g., zero-shot, few-shot), refinement-based (e.g., RCI, self-refine), decomposition-based (e.g., least-to-most, self-planning), reasoning-based (e.g., CoT, zero-shot CoT, self-consistency), and priming (persona/memetic proxy).  The study highlights that prior code-generation research overwhelmingly uses only zero-/few-shot prompting, overlooking richer strategies.

Using the LLMSecEval dataset (150 natural-language prompts covering 18 CWE categories), the authors evaluate selected non-demonstrative techniques—zero-shot, zero-shot CoT, persona/memetic proxy, Recursive Criticism & Improvement (RCI), and progressive-hint prompting—on GPT-3, GPT-3.5, and GPT-4. Security is assessed using Bandit and CodeQL.

Results show that while LLM-generated code contains numerous vulnerabilities (e.g., CWE-78, CWE-259, CWE-330), prompting strategies can significantly reduce these weaknesses, with RCI consistently providing the strongest improvements, especially for GPT-4. Persona-based prompting also introduces beneficial behavioral shifts. Overall, the paper provides the first systematic evidence that prompt design meaningfully influences secure code generation, offering a taxonomy, reusable templates, and guidance for future research.
